insert into public.articles (slug, title, excerpt, body_md, tags, min_level, published_at)
values
  ('telling-a-child-someone-has-died', 'Telling a Child Someone Has Died', 'You have to say it and you do not know how. The words that work, the ones to avoid, and what children of different ages tend to need afterward.', 'You are standing in the hall working out how to walk into the room. Somebody has died, the children do not know yet, and you are the one who has to say it.

There is no version of this that does not hurt. There is a version that gives them something solid to stand on, and that is what you are aiming for.

Do it soon, and do it yourself

Children almost always know something is wrong before they are told. They hear phone calls, they see faces, and in the gap they usually invent something worse or decide it is their fault.

So tell them early, in a quiet place, with the television off, from somebody they trust rather than from a relative they barely know.

If you can, have one other adult there who can take over if you cannot speak. And take two minutes beforehand to steady yourself: not to hide your feelings, but so you can get the sentence out.

Use the plain words

This is the part adults most want to avoid, and the one that matters most: say died.

Gone away, passed, lost, asleep, gone to a better place. Young children take all of those literally, and the results are real: looking for the person, being frightened of sleep, waiting for someone to come back from a trip.

Something like this works: I have something very sad to tell you. Grandad died this morning. His body stopped working and he will not be coming back. I am so sorry.

Then stop talking. Let the silence sit, however uncomfortable it is, and let them react however they do.

Expect any reaction at all

Children grieve in bursts, which startles adults who expect sustained sadness.

They may cry. They may say nothing. They may ask whether they can go and play, or what is for dinner, and then be devastated at bedtime. They may be furious. Many ask something that sounds callous, like who gets the dog, which is simply a child trying to work out what changes for them.

None of that means they do not care. A child can only hold a big feeling for a short while, so they dip in and out of it for weeks.

Answer the questions, including the hard ones

Children ask directly, and they need honest, short answers.

Will you die too? Everybody dies one day, and I expect to be here for a very long time. That is honest and reassuring without a promise you cannot make.

Was it my fault? No. Nothing you did, said or thought caused this.

How did they die? Give a true, simple version at their level, and expect to repeat it. If the death was sudden, violent, or by suicide, say the simple true thing — his body was too badly hurt, or his illness made him so unwell that he ended his own life — and get advice from a bereavement service about how much to say at their age.

And it is fine to say I do not know. Children trust that more than invented certainty.

Say what happens next

Most of a child''s fear is about the ordinary machinery of life, not the philosophy.

So tell them the practical things. Who will take you to school. Where you will sleep. Whether the house changes. Whether you still go on the trip. What a funeral is, and whether they can come, which is usually better offered than imposed.

Repeat it over the days that follow. Children absorb this in pieces, and they will ask the same question four times.

Expect it in their behavior

Grief in children often shows up as behavior rather than words: clinginess, going backward in sleep or toileting, tummy aches, tantrums, trouble at school, falling asleep in class, suddenly being a perfectionist.

Teenagers tend to go to their rooms, to their friends, or to their phones, and may look unaffected while being nothing of the kind.

So keep routines going, because predictability is what holds children together. Tell the school on day one. Keep bedtime the same. Keep the expectations roughly the same, with more patience built in.

Let them see you upset

Children learn what to do with feelings by watching.

If you hide all of it, they conclude they must hide theirs too. If they see you cry and also see you make dinner, they learn that sadness is survivable.

What they should not carry is responsibility for your grief. So cry in front of them and get your own comfort from other adults, not from your child.

And name the person often. Children worry that the dead are never mentioned again. Telling stories, laughing at a memory, keeping a photograph where they can see it: all of that helps more than careful silence.

When to get help

Please talk to your child''s doctor or school if, after several weeks, your child cannot sleep, will not eat, is not playing or engaging at all, is frightened to leave you, has stopped going to school, or seems to be getting worse rather than slowly settling.

Ask early rather than late if they are harming themselves, talking about not wanting to be here, or saying they want to be with the person who died; speak to a doctor the same day.

Child bereavement services exist in most places, will see families without a referral, and are extremely good at exactly this. A counselor can also help you with the part of this that is yours, which is often where the real exhaustion sits.

And if you are the adult holding everyone together and it has taken you somewhere dark, please tell a doctor or someone you trust today. In the US you can call or text 988 at any time.', array['Loss', 'Parenting']::text[], 0, '2026-10-20T14:00:00+00:00'::timestamptz),
  ('after-a-panic-attack-in-public', 'After a Panic Attack in Public', 'It happened in the supermarket, or the meeting, and people saw. How to handle the hours afterward, and what to do so the place does not become somewhere you avoid.', 'You were in line at the store, or halfway through the presentation, and your body decided this was an emergency. Someone fetched you a chair. Someone else asked if you wanted water.

It passed, as it always does. And now you are in the car park replaying it, and the worst part is not the panic. It is that people watched.

Two different problems

The panic itself is one thing, and it is over.

The second thing is the aftermath: the hot shame, the certainty that you made a scene, and the urge to never go back to that shop, that office, that train.

Those need separate handling, and the second one is what decides how much of your life this costs you.

What people actually saw

Almost always less than you think.

Panic is enormously loud from the inside and surprisingly quiet from the outside. What people generally see is somebody going pale, breathing fast, sitting down, maybe leaving. Not the roaring in your ears, not the conviction that you were dying.

And what they mostly felt was concern, briefly, followed by getting on with their day. The person who brought the chair has told the story once, as a kind thing they did, and forgotten it.

That is not reassurance for its own sake. It is a correction to the version your mind is running, which is filmed from the ceiling with everyone staring.

Handle the hours afterward deliberately

Panic leaves a physical hangover: shaky, exhausted, faintly ill, with a mind that wants to replay.

So treat the next hour as recovery. Water, something to eat, somewhere to sit, a walk if you can manage one. Do not go straight into something demanding and do not drive while still trembling.

Then put a limit on the replaying. One pass, with a pen if it helps: where it happened, what led up to it, what you did, how long it lasted. That is the useful information. The twentieth pass is not analysis, it is punishment.

What to say to people

The dread of the conversation is often worse than the conversation.

Short and matter-of-fact works best: sorry about yesterday, I had a panic attack, they are horrible and harmless, I am fine. Then change the subject.

You do not owe anyone an explanation of your history, and you do not have to apologize at length for having a body. If somebody was kind, thank them once.

With a manager or a colleague, you can keep it to capacity: I was unwell for a few minutes, I am all right, and I may need a couple of small adjustments. That is enough.

Expect the shame to lift before the fear does

Most people find the embarrassment fades within days, especially once they have had one ordinary interaction with somebody who was there.

The fear of a repeat lasts longer, and it is the thing that shapes behavior. Watch for it: taking the long way round, going at quiet times, needing somebody with you, sitting near the door, carrying water you do not drink, keeping your phone in your hand.

Each of those gives relief and quietly teaches your alarm that the place was genuinely dangerous.

Go back sooner than you want to

This is the part that decides the next six months, and it is uncomfortable.

Go back to the same place, soon — within days rather than weeks — and keep the visit short. Then again. Then again, a little longer, at a busier hour, without the person who came with you.

Repetition is what updates the alarm. One brave visit followed by three weeks of avoidance teaches it nothing.

And drop the props gradually rather than all at once. The order usually goes: quiet hour with a friend, quiet hour alone, busy hour with a friend, busy hour alone.

If it happens again

Assume it might, and have a short plan so it is not a crisis.

Breathe out longer than you breathe in. Feet on the floor, hand on something solid. Say the flat sentence: this is panic, it peaks in a few minutes, it has never hurt me.

Stay where you are if you possibly can, because leaving at the peak is what teaches escape. Sitting down is fine. Letting somebody fetch you water is fine.

And if a stranger helps you, let them. Most people are glad to be useful, and accepting help is not a debt.

When to get help with it

Please talk to a doctor if you are not sure the symptoms are panic, particularly if you fainted, had chest pain with exertion, or felt confused; that deserves checking once.

And talk to a doctor or a therapist if panic is happening regularly, if you have started avoiding places, or if the dread of it is shaping where you go and what you say yes to. Panic responds very well to treatment, usually in a small number of sessions, and the method is largely what is above, done in order with someone keeping you honest about the avoidance.

If the shame is the loudest part — if you cannot face people who were there, or you have withdrawn since — say that plainly to the doctor or therapist you see. It is a common and treatable piece of this rather than a character flaw, and if it has taken you somewhere dark, please tell someone you trust today. In the US you can call or text 988 at any time.', array['Panic', 'Shame']::text[], 0, '2026-10-20T14:00:00+00:00'::timestamptz),
  ('being-kind-to-yourself-without-lying-to-yourself', 'Being Kind to Yourself Without Lying to Yourself', 'Positive affirmations feel false and self-criticism feels true. A middle option: saying the accurate thing about how you feel, which works better than either.', 'You have tried the kind version. I am doing my best, I am enough, be gentle with yourself. And something in you answers immediately: no I am not, that is nonsense, you dropped the ball on Tuesday.

So you go back to the other voice, the one that calls you useless, because at least that one sounds like it is telling the truth.

Why the nice sentences bounce off

Reassurance only lands when you already half believe it. Said against a strong belief, it starts an argument, and you lose arguments with yourself.

Worse, when the kind sentence is plainly untrue — I am great at this, when you have just made a mess of it — your own mind flags it as false, and the whole exercise feels embarrassing.

So the problem is not that kindness does not work. It is that most kind sentences people are handed are claims about your worth, and claims can be disputed.

The alternative is accuracy

There is a middle between flattery and attack, and it is simply describing what is actually happening.

This is hard, and it makes sense that I am struggling. I am exhausted, which is why this feels impossible. Anyone would find that conversation frightening. I did badly at this and I was also doing it with no sleep.

Notice what those have in common: nothing in them is deniable. They are not compliments. They name a situation and grant that your reaction fits it, which is the thing people are actually short of.

Validation, in the technical sense, is not saying good job. It is saying: that follows.

How to say it in practice

Three steps, and you can do them in thirty seconds.

Name the feeling plainly. I am ashamed. I am furious. I am frightened about Friday.

Then find the reason it makes sense, without arguing the facts. Of course I am ashamed, I care about doing this well and it went wrong in front of people.

Then say what is also true. And it was one meeting, and I can fix the part that was mine.

That last step is where the balance lives: it does not cancel the feeling, and it does not let the feeling have the whole floor.

What to do when it was your fault

This is where people get stuck, because self-compassion sounds like letting yourself off.

It is not. The accurate version includes the part you got wrong: I was short with him, that was not fair, and I was running on four hours of sleep. All three facts are true at once, and the middle one does not require the self-attack.

In fact the self-attack usually gets in the way of repair. People in a shame spiral avoid the person they hurt; people who can say I got that wrong, plainly, actually apologize.

So the test is not whether a sentence is nice. It is whether it moves you toward doing something useful.

Say it as you would to a friend

Not the words a friend would say to flatter you, the words a good friend would actually use.

A good friend does not say you are perfect. They say: of course you are upset, that was a horrible week, and you still managed to get the kids to school. Warmth and accuracy together.

If you cannot find that voice for yourself, borrow it. Write what you would say to somebody you like who was in exactly your position, then read it back addressed to you. People find this uncomfortable and effective.

Keep a few true sentences ready

Under pressure you will not compose anything, so have three or four lines prepared and written down.

This is hard and it makes sense that it is hard. I have got through worse than this. I do not have to feel better to do the next thing. Being tired is not a character flaw. This will look smaller on Thursday.

Flat, factual, no flattery. Say them out loud if you can; something about hearing them helps more than thinking them.

Expect it to feel odd before it feels useful

If the critical voice has run things for years, the accurate voice will sound weak at first, and you may notice a small panic that without the harshness you will stop trying.

That fear is common and it is not borne out. Self-criticism is a poor long-term motivator — it tends to produce avoidance rather than effort — while being able to say this is hard and I am still going is what keeps people working at things.

Give it a few weeks of unimpressive practice. It is a skill rather than a conversion.

When to get help with it

Please talk to a therapist or a counselor if the voice in your head is harsh in a way you would never use with anyone else, if nothing you do ever counts, or if the self-attack has gone on so long that you cannot remember another way of speaking to yourself.

This is one of the most practical areas in therapy, and it responds well: a counselor will work on exactly these sentences with you and keep you honest about the ones you skip. And if the self-criticism has come with a mood that has stayed low for weeks, mention that to a doctor too; it is common, treatable, and much easier to work on with help than alone.', array['DBT', 'Self-Compassion']::text[], 0, '2026-10-20T14:00:00+00:00'::timestamptz),
  ('being-bad-at-resting', 'Being Bad at Resting', 'You sit down and last four minutes before finding something to do. Why rest feels so uncomfortable for some people, and how to get better at it without turning it into a project.', 'You have an afternoon with nothing in it. By quarter past two you have cleaned something, answered two emails and looked at the garden assessingly, and the sitting down has not happened.

When you do stop, you feel vaguely guilty, slightly itchy, and strangely tired without being rested.

Why stopping is uncomfortable

For a lot of people, being busy is not a schedule. It is a way of feeling all right about themselves.

If your sense of being worth something is tied to being useful, then an hour of doing nothing is an hour with no evidence in it, and the discomfort that follows is not laziness avoided; it is the absence of something you rely on.

There is often a history: a house where rest had to be earned, a parent who was always doing, years of work where only output counted. That teaches a rule nobody ever says out loud, which is that you are allowed to stop once everything is finished — and everything is never finished.

And stopping lets things catch up with you. Grief, dread, the conversation you have not had. Busy is an extremely effective way of not feeling something, which is why the first quiet hour is often the hardest.

Notice what you do instead

Most people in this position do not rest, they switch tasks.

Scrolling, which is stimulation rather than rest. Tidying. Admin. A podcast while cleaning while planning the week. Working out hard enough that it is another item on the list.

None of those are bad. They are not rest, though, and a day full of them will leave you depleted while looking productive.

The test is simple: afterward, do you feel more capable or less?

Define what rest means for you

Rest is not one thing, and the version everyone recommends may not be yours.

Some people need stillness: lying down, staring out of a window, a bath, sitting in the garden.

Some need absorption: a book, a jigsaw, cooking something slow, a long walk, playing an instrument badly. The mind rests when it is occupied by something undemanding.

Some need company, and some need nobody at all. Some need to be outside.

Write down the three things that genuinely leave you feeling better afterward. That list is more useful than any general advice, and it is probably shorter than you expect.

Give it a start and an end

This is the trick that makes it possible for people who cannot simply relax.

Open-ended rest is uncomfortable, so put edges on it. Twenty minutes. Until the kettle boils twice. One episode. Until three o''clock. A timer, oddly, makes it feel permitted.

And schedule it like a commitment rather than waiting for a gap, because the gap does not come. Two half-hours in the diary with a name beats a vague intention to take it easy at the weekend.

Expect the first ten minutes to be unpleasant

The itchiness is the part people take as evidence that rest does not suit them.

It is not. It is what happens when an activated system slows down, plus a bit of withdrawal from the usefulness you were running on. It passes, usually somewhere in the second ten minutes.

So decide in advance that you will stay put through the fidgeting. Put the phone in another room, because that is what the itch reaches for.

And if something underneath comes up when you stop — a feeling, a problem you have been outrunning — that is information rather than a reason to get up. Note it and look at it later, on purpose.

Deal with the guilt out loud

The guilt arrives with sentences, so answer them with plainer ones.

I should be doing something. Rest is what makes the something possible. I have not earned this. Nobody earns rest; it is maintenance, like sleep. Other people manage more. Other people are not doing my week.

It also helps enormously to see other people rest without apology. If everyone around you is busy at all times, notice that and be the person who sits down.

Do not optimize it

The final trap, especially for perfectionists: turning rest into a performance with a routine, an app and a morning schedule.

If your recovery has milestones, it is another project. Keep it unimpressive. Nothing to track, nothing to be good at, no before-and-after.

The measure of a restful hour is simply that the next hour went better.

When to get help

Please talk to a doctor if you are exhausted in a way that rest does not touch, if you have been running on empty for months, if you are not sleeping, or if you cannot stop without feeling panicky.

A therapist is the right place for the part underneath — the belief that you are only all right while you are useful, which is common, understandable and very workable. And if being still has become unbearable because of what comes up when you are quiet, say that plainly to a counselor; that is a specific, treatable thing rather than a personality trait, and it is one of the better reasons to make an appointment.', array['Perfectionism', 'Self-Care']::text[], 0, '2026-10-20T14:00:00+00:00'::timestamptz),
  ('when-you-get-knocked-back-again', 'When You Get Knocked Back Again', 'The fortieth application, the fourth audition, another no. How to keep going through repeated rejection without deciding it is a verdict on you.', 'The email starts with thank you for your interest. You already know. That is the ninth one, or the thirtieth, and this one you actually thought you had.

The first few were disappointing. Somewhere around the twentieth, something changed, and it stopped feeling like bad luck and started feeling like information about you.

What repetition does to the meaning

One rejection is an event. A long run of them turns into a story, and the story is almost always about your worth rather than about the process.

That shift happens quietly. I did not get that job becomes they can tell, becomes there is something wrong with me that other people can see and I cannot.

It helps to know this is what volume does to a person, rather than what the evidence supports. The same forty applications would produce the same feeling in anybody.

Separate the numbers from the verdict

Most of these processes have ratios that nobody tells you about.

Hiring is largely a matter of fit, timing and internal candidates. Auditions are cast for a look and a tone you cannot control. Dating is a numbers business with chemistry in it. Funding, publishing and grants reject the overwhelming majority of good applications.

So the honest reading of thirty nos in those systems is usually: this is what thirty attempts looks like. Not: thirty people assessed my value and agreed.

Writing down the actual ratio — how many attempts a result usually takes in your field — is worth more than any pep talk.

Ask for one piece of feedback, then stop asking

Feedback is useful once. After that it tends to become a way of searching for the flaw.

So ask one or two places, specifically: is there anything I could do differently next time? Most will not answer. The ones who do often say something small and fixable, and occasionally something that reframes the whole thing.

Then act on what is actionable and leave the rest. If three people say the same thing, that is a pattern worth work. One person''s remark is one person''s remark.

Count what you control

The outcome is not in your hands, so measure the parts that are.

Applications sent. Approaches made. Hours practiced. One new contact a week. A better version of the covering letter. A different route entirely.

Keep a tally of those and let it be the scoreboard, because a scoreboard of outcomes in a high-rejection process is designed to make you feel like a failure.

And set a quota rather than a goal: ten a week, three a day, whatever is sustainable. Quotas survive a bad week; hopes do not.

Protect the hour after a no

The damage usually happens in the hour afterward, not in the email.

So have a plan. Read it once, note anything useful, close it. Then do something physical for twenty minutes: a walk, the stairs, something heavy. Tell one person, briefly, who will not try to fix it.

Do not reply at length, do not reread your application looking for the mistake, and do not send the next one while raw, because it will show.

And give it a time limit. Twenty minutes of being properly fed up is healthy. Three days of it is where the story takes hold.

Keep something in your life that is not this

Long rejection runs take over, and that makes every single no heavier, because there is nothing else in the day to weigh against it.

So keep something with reliable returns in the week: exercise, a class, a craft, volunteering, time with people who like you for reasons unrelated to this. Something where effort and outcome are connected, which is exactly what the search is not.

And protect the relationships. People in a long stretch of this tend to withdraw out of embarrassment, which removes the thing that holds them up.

Reassess on a schedule, not on a bad day

There is a real question in here: how long do I keep going, and should I change the approach?

Answer it deliberately, every month or two, with somebody sensible, rather than at eleven at night after a rejection. On a bad night the answer is always that you should give up, and that answer is not information.

And consider that changing the approach is not quitting. A different sector, a different city, a different level, a course, a different kind of application: those are moves, not surrender.

When to get help with it

Please talk to a doctor or a therapist if the low mood has been there for weeks rather than days, if you are not sleeping or eating properly, if you have stopped applying altogether, or if the nos have hardened into a settled belief that you are worthless.

That is extremely common in long rejection stretches, it is treatable, and it is much easier to keep going once it lifts. A counselor can also help with the particular habit of reading every outcome as a verdict on you, which is specific, well understood and workable.

And if it has taken you somewhere dark, and you are not sure you want to be here, please tell a doctor or someone you trust today rather than carrying it alone.', array['Resilience', 'Self-Esteem']::text[], 0, '2026-10-20T14:00:00+00:00'::timestamptz)
